<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bubastis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell Basta, Per-Bast</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bubastis was a city in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, at Tell Basta near modern Zagazig, and the cult centre of the cat goddess Bastet. Libyan chiefs of the Meshwesh settled there, and their descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a> founded the Twenty-second Dynasty, whose kings enlarged its temple. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described its festival of Bastet as the most popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and many mummified cats were buried in its cemeteries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bubastis.jpg" alt="Bubastis" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Einsamer Schütze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bubastis_011.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bubastis, known in Egyptian as Per-Bast, the House of Bast, lay along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in the Delta region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and was the capital of its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a>, Am-Khent, the 18th of Lower Egypt. It stood on the Pelusiac branch of the Nile near the entrance to the Wadi Tumilat, which made it a hub for trade and for expeditions to the Sinai and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Its ruins are in the suburbs of the modern city of Zagazig. It is often identified with the biblical Pi-Beseth. The settlement began at the end of the 4th millennium BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The cult of Bastet is known from the reign of Hotepsekhemwy in the Second Dynasty. In the Fourth Dynasty a governor's palace was built, and in the Sixth Dynasty there were two temples for the royal Ka of Teti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-i">Pepi I</a>. Middle Kingdom kings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a>, contributed to the sanctuary, and in the Twelfth Dynasty a large palace for the governors was built. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> expanded the Temple of Bastet, and much of its red granite bears his cartouches; a colossal statue of his daughter-wife Meritamun is among the site's finds.</p><p>The city became a royal residence after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> in 943 BCE and founded the Twenty-second Dynasty, and it was at its height under that dynasty and the 23rd. Near the city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a> began, but never finished, the Canal of the Pharaohs between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. It declined after the conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> in 525 BCE, when its walls were dismantled. Three Christian bishops of Bubastis are known from the 4th and 5th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bastet was at first shown as a lioness and later became associated with the cat, and the tombs at Bubastis were the principal depository in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> of cat mummies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>, and her oracle grew in popularity after Greek settlers came to the Delta. Herodotus called her festival the most joyous of the Egyptian calendar, with barges of pilgrims singing and playing music down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, and said that as many as hundreds of thousands of pilgrims had attended; he also described the temple as surrounded by two canals and lined with trees.</p><p>Excavations include the discovery of the tomb of the vizier Iuty in 1964, a well preserved copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decree-of-canopus">Decree of Canopus</a> in 2004, and the German-Egyptian Tell Basta Project since 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bubastis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sheshonq I, Great Chief of the Ma and descendant of Meshwesh settlers at <b>Bubastis</b>, succeeded the Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt around 943 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-second-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-second Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
